{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; sed -n '270,350p' AGENTS.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; sed -n '270,350p' AGENTS.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n\nAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n\n| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n\n## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n\n- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n  system (D263).\n- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n  competition or pricing\".\n- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n  of the previous filing topic).\n- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n\n## 5. What to do\n\nStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n\n1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n     the existing rule).\n   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n- **A ruler or gold that is built and reviewed is never changed again** (D261). Reviewed means readers who did not write it have read it\n  against the decisions and Alex has signed it. A ruler under construction is edited only until that review, and nothing is scored\n  against it before.\n- **The ruler is Alex's, never your own.** Gold, unit and the judge's reading are checked against what the product uses and fixed with\n  Alex before the first arm. A standard or reference you write gives every sentence its source (the signed standard, a decision, a PRD)\n  or lists it for Alex's decision with a lean; settling wording is no licence to change behaviour. After each rewrite, a read-only Codex\n  review against the decisions looks for unsourced rules, contradictions and misquotes, round after round until only wording-level\n  findings remain; clarity is tested apart, by independent readers on cases fixed before the text changes. A failed registered acceptance\n  stops the line and goes to Alex with its census; the next arm is never designed alone.\n- **Nobody grades their own change with a ruler still being corrected.** The reference is one Alex can read, reviewed and frozen before\n  the arms; a number scored against a ruler its author keeps correcting is not a conclusion. Meaning is judged by a reader who did not\n  write the change; code checks only structure and counts (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"我根本无法相信你的评价\").\n- **A new gold** (only for a new ruler version Alex opens). The unit is fixed before the first send (for extraction, one research matter\n  in one article, which several records may express; signed standard §11) and the gold is written from the complete material the model\n  sees before any answer is read. Each item holds the exact source span (checked by code), the standard line it follows, its weight under the signed rule and, where\n  proposals are pending, its weight under them with the proposals it depends on named.\n  A blind second reader reads the same sources; every disagreement is decided against the source and its reason recorded. The gold is\n  frozen with its sha256 and never edited after a send. Model-authored gold and model judges are labelled as such. A held-out set no arm\n  has seen is in from the first arm.\n- **Measure the stick before using it:** control against a second control, the criterion's null estimated (a conjunction over many items\n  fails by chance), and its workload sized from its rules. Tune on dev; score test once per configuration.\n\n### 7.2 Reading results\n\n- **A score is a lead; the traces are the measurement.** Every measurement has a score, and every time you read the traces yourself: the\n  raw answer beside the article for every missed helpful entry, and a sample of the extras and of the hits; historical gold keeps its original labels. The scorer\n  hard-codes no rule of the standard: it reads the gold file it is given, prints the gold's and the standard's sha256, and refuses a gold\n  made for another version; scores are compared only on the same gold. Do not write a new scorer to judge a probe: read the traces yourself (Alex, 2026-11-08:\n  \"不要写评分器这个之前就出问题过，你自己读trace 来分析\"). Alex, 2026-10-29 (D244): \"我觉得这是最容易出错的问题；你非常容易写死，我们在改的过程中如果drift你会一直用久的标准来衡量，这边需要有打分，但是“一定”要你自己下去看trace这是必须，记起来\".\n- **The ruler counts only what it was built to count.** Before a quality claim, a reader who did not write the change also reads the\n  fields the product stores (original day, original speaker, tier, the summary's hedges) and what the next step does with the record\n  (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"现在到底质量怎么样你深度的解析不要只是看尺子\").\n- Read the judges' reasons, not only the scores, and pick the next fix from the largest cause. Open the samples behind a metric's largest\n  values before reading it, and write down what a derived number reduces to.\n- **Report every number, regressions first.** For future evidence evaluation, combine the existing gold's core and supporting entries as helpful, use its not-kept entries as unhelpful, and leave optional entries neutral (D372). Count one research matter in one article (D250), not raw sentences. Retain the old labels and scores in historical results, and do not silently relabel the frozen gold. One quantity per table column. A comparison of\n  totals uses three draws; a count from one draw is labelled one draw and shows a mechanism, not a difference between arms. Lead with the\n  mechanism and the share of its class it explains, then the fix.\n- **Read what was dropped.** A statement the pipeline drops is thrown away with its reason, never parked on a side list; evaluation\n  reads the dropped statements by hand, classifies them by the mechanism that would have kept them, and fixes the largest class upstream\n  (D209: \"if it doesn't matter, just fucking throw it away … if there are false positives that got thrown away … you got to pick it up\").\n- **Measure end to end before a step's arm, and read the parked designs first.** A step's noise that the next step removes is not the\n  defect, and a step's recall that the next step throws away is. Before any arm on one step: the production replay through the whole path\n  on the gold window, the count at the step the product reads, and the decisions on the topic read for a parked fix (Alex, 2026-10-31:\n  \"make sure we are not going around in circles using wrong ruler and wrong prompts that don't work\"). Before a phase targets one step,\n  the upside of that step done perfectly is measured where the product is judged: the judge is given the gold's own evidence, built by\n  the production code, a few sends (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"我很不开心你的失败，为什么，深度多轮检讨记录，想思路突破\").\n- **Read what was kept against the gold, blind, before the next noise arm.** The not-kept gold entries a version keeps are judged by a\n  reader who wrote neither the gold nor the prompt, mixed with controls it cannot tell apart; where it parts from the gold, the standard\n  is unclear or the gold misreads it, and that goes to Alex before any arm counts it as noise (Alex, 2026-10-31: \"你再次重新检查尺子，看看是不是这些留下的还有歧义；然后再思考怎么降低noise\").\n- Pair every structural check with its semantic one. Run a data rule over its whole input range and on cases it was not tuned on. Weigh\n  a case by what it carries in the product, not by count.\n- A claim about a population (\"the data is clean\") rests on a code sweep of the whole population and an independent read of a fresh\n  random sample, never on the pages the author looked at.\n- Cost is priced from tokens at the published rates (`hivemind.model.PRICES`, cached input at `model.CACHED_INPUT_SHARE`), never from a\n  stored `cost_usd`; say which rates a projection uses.\n\n### 7.3 Iterating\n\n- **One class at a time** (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"). Count the misses by mechanism; take the largest\n  class; trace it to the sentence that causes it; change one thing aimed at that class; run the whole bench (a prompt change alters every\n  request) against the previous version; read every regression to its raw answer. The pages that showed the failure locate its\n  mechanism; only the whole bench (every set, every draw) accepts the change.\n- **Name the effect on the judgement first.** Every proposed change states the effect it is expected to have on a reader or on the final\n  judgement, and is tested there where possible; a step's own counts do not decide it alone. A fix that keeps moving its error to\n  neighbouring cases stops being tuned on that set (Alex, 2026-11-09: \"感觉现在一直在修一些mknor的原地打转，你是不是要思考下现在具体还差什么high priority big impact 的需要做？\";\n  he approved this rule the same day, D391).\n- **A model call is a sampler.** Re-send a stored request before calling its answer a defect. Totals and acceptance come from at least\n  three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A direct-read pass\n  (one send per article, every answer read beside its source; D238) locates mechanisms; its counts are one draw and are labelled so.\n- **A written log.** One row per arm: hypothesis, change, numbers against the control (regressions first), kept or not. Refuted arms and\n  your own mistakes stay in it; the next session starts from its last row.\n- **Own the loop, within what Alex agreed.** When the direction is agreed and no stop is in force (`CURRENT_STATE.md`), push your own\n  hypotheses through $0 checks and probes of a few cents on stored cases without asking. A registered test, an end-to-end run, or\n  anything that departs from the agreed plan starts on his word; a result that contradicts the hypothesis stops the line and goes to him\n  (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\"). While he is not active (D439), \"his word\" is the council's\n  recorded consensus, and a stopped line goes to the council for re-assessment.\n- **Council before every change is sent.** A prompt, schema, code or criterion change, including one found by reading a probe, is a new\n  arm. It goes to an independent Codex read and a challenge round before its first send, and its result goes to a challenge round\n  before the next change. Reading the traces and the model's own account locates a fix; it does not review it (Alex, 2026-11-08:\n  \"你这些改动有我你有好好的跑council 再下手\").\n  Every round's reasoning and decisions are reflected on in the council until there is a consensus; the decision is recorded, and\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13: related number questions judged together, with a checked calculation (design v10, after council round 46j and D728; for round 46k)\n     2\t\n     3\tOpened by Alex (D726) for the bounded test of D725. Harness-only until a later decision: no product configuration names it, and\n     4\tthe ordinary launchers refuse it (D359, D723). The frozen judge (judging-v7, poc-v118) is unchanged.\n     5\t\n     6\tv2 answered round 46 (`codex-r46.md`, changes marked [46.n]). v3 answers round 46b (`codex-r46b.md`, marked [46b.n]). Rounds 46 and\n     7\t46b found the same weakness five times: the checks trusted what the answer declares about itself (its `uses` list, its labels, a\n     8\tshortened span). **v3's structural rule:** code derives every relation it checks from the calculation itself and from the passage\n     9\ttext, and the answer declares nothing code relies on without checking it:\n    10\t- each outcome's inputs are found by walking the figure graph;\n    11\t- a future quantity shared by the family enters any forecast only through the family's one forecast node for it, checked on every\n    12\t  path from an outcome, not by membership in the graph [46c.1];\n    13\t- a source figure must equal a complete numeric expression that code extracts from the passage.\n    14\t\n    15\t## 1. What changes\n    16\t\n    17\t| Piece | judging-v7 (today) | judging-v13 |\n    18\t|---|---|---|\n    19\t| Unit | one question per call | one **family** per call: the number questions of one company whose quantities overlap (a total and its segment or product line, a level and its growth, and a later year declared to rest on an earlier year's base [46.2]). Each keeps its own answer |\n    20\t| Who forms a family | none (0 dependencies) | **declared** for the test from the stored cases, with typed relations: `part_of(part, whole)` and `same_base(later, earlier)`. Production would need a matching version that names them |\n    21\t| Evidence shown | each question's own evidence in view (D198) | the union of the members' evidence in view, each passage once with its attribution; themes once |\n    22\t| Previous conclusion | rung and reasons, cut at 800 characters, unmarked | **not shown** (D728). Whether a member's conclusion changed is derived by code afterwards from its earlier rung, outcome and source figures (`calc_check.changed`) |\n    23\t| Answer | prose fields plus one answer-wide citation list | a calculation (figures) plus exactly one answer per member (§2) |\n    24\t| Request text | judging-v7 | a full v13 request written and audited (§5) [46.6] |\n    25\t| Failure | refused twice: the question is left as it was | refused twice: the family is recorded **unresolved** and no member is updated. A member may also answer `unresolved` itself (§2) [46.5] |\n    26\t\n    27\t## 2. The answer format (closed set; the schema enforces shape, code enforces §3)\n    28\t\n    29\t```\n    30\tfigures: [ { id,\n    31\t             what: { population, measure, scope, period },     # canonical: measure is the quantity (\"revenue\"), not its basis [46.1]\n    32\t             basis: \"reported\" | \"guidance\" | \"consensus\" | \"third_party\" | \"forecast\" | \"prior_forecast\",\n    33\t             scenario: \"base\" | \"<named>\",                       # default \"base\" [46.1]\n    34\t             kind: \"source\" | \"computed\" | \"estimate\",\n    35\t             value | {low, high} | {bound: \">\" | \">=\" | \"<\" | \"<=\" | \"approx\" | \"approx_below\" | \"approx_above\", value} | {multiplier, bound?},  unit,   # [46c.3]\n    36\t             # kind source:   evidence: \"<id>\", expression: <index of a complete expression code extracts from that passage>,\n    37\t             #                component: \"midpoint\" | \"low\" | \"high\" | \"bound\" | \"multiplier\" | \"value\"            [46b.2]\n    38\t             # kind computed: inputs: [...], operation: sum|difference|product|ratio|growth|apply_growth|share|range_from_relative|roll_forward,   # [46c.2]\n    39\t             #                relation (sum and roll_forward): \"accounting_parts\" | \"time_aggregation\" | \"stated_in_addition\" | \"overlap_unknown\",\n    40\t             #                disputed (overlap_unknown only): \"<the operand whose inclusion is in doubt>\"            [46b.4, 46d]\n    41\t             #                bridge (when inputs' what differ from the output's): \"<figure id of an estimate>\"   [46.3]\n    42\t             # kind estimate: basis_text, rests_on: [figure ids, evidence ids],\n    43\t             #                adjusts (optional): { figure: \"<id>\", type: \"discount\" | \"uplift\" | \"trim\" }            [46.3]\n    44\t             #                a bridge estimate also names `converts: [operand ids]` and the assumption           [46b.3]\n    45\t           } ]\n    46\tanswers: [ { view_id, shape: \"forecast\", outcome: \"<figure id>\", rung,                 # [46b.5] two shapes\n    47\t             reference: { figure: \"<id>\" } | { unavailable: \"<why>\" },                # per answer [46.2]\n    48\t             reasons, cites, pushes, theme_pushes, changed, why_changed }\n    49\t         | { view_id, shape: \"unresolved\", why, cites, changed, why_changed } ]       # no outcome, no rung\n    50\t```\n    51\t\n    52\t- **An arithmetic intermediate and the analyst's final range are distinct figures** [46.2]. For example, q02's $392B × 92% is a\n    53\t  `computed` figure. The final $370–400B is an `estimate` resting on it and on q01's range. Code checks the intermediate's arithmetic\n    54\t  and never requires the final range to equal it.\n    55\t- **`prior`** figures (basis `prior_forecast`) are for comparison only [46f.4]. They need a pointer to an earlier answer shown in the\n    56\t  request and must equal a figure that answer states (C10). They may feed no figure. A declared `same_base` reads the family's own\n    57\t  forecast node of the earlier member at the same snapshot (q22 reads q01's): C9.\n    58\t- **`roll_forward`** [46c.2]: an opening balance as of a date, plus additions and minus drawdowns over a later period, gives the\n    59\t  balance at that period's end. It is the accounting identity for a stock and its flows. An addition whose inclusion in the opening\n    60\t  balance is in doubt carries `relation: overlap_unknown` and `disputed` (R2's $18B).\n    61\t- **Scenarios carry ranges** [46b.1]. A forecast node is a point within a scenario. A range across members is written as paired\n    62\t  scenarios (for example `low`, `high`) whose nodes stay paired. The analyst's final answer may be a range `estimate` resting on the\n    63\t  scenario outcomes.\n    64\t- **The family declaration** (by the harness, not by the answer) lists the shared quantities and periods the members depend on, for\n    65\t  example company revenue for each FY2027 quarter, and the typed relations [46b.1].\n    66\t\n    67\t## 3. What code checks (identity and arithmetic only)\n    68\t\n    69\t| Check | Rule | Refuses |\n    70\t|---|---|---|\n    71\t| **C1 source expression** [46b.2] | code extracts every complete numeric expression from the cited passage as shown, with its qualifiers and unit. Points: \"$108.0 billion\". Ranges: \"$105–110 billion\". Relative: \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" is midpoint 108 ±2%. Bounds: \"more than $730 billion\" (>730), \"at least\" (>=). Approximate bounds: \"nearly $800 billion\" (approx_below 800). Multipliers: \"double\" ×2, \"more than double\" (>×2). A source figure names one extracted expression and a component of it (midpoint, low, high, bound, multiplier), and must equal it exactly: value, unit, sign and qualifier. Any other wording cannot be a source figure | a figure that strips a qualifier ($730 from \"more than $730 billion\"); a bound used as a point; a wrong number within 0.5% (108.5); a figure naming no extracted expression |\n    72\t| **C2 arithmetic** | a `computed` figure recomputes from its inputs by its operation. Rounding: to the figure's stated precision, at most 0.5% or 0.1 unit. `range_from_relative` makes 108 ± 2% into 105.84–110.16 | a wrong sum, share or endpoint |\n    73\t| **C3 part within whole** [46b.1] | for each declared `part_of`, in each scenario, for each declared period: the part's value, found by walking the graph from the part member's outcome to its node for that period, is at most the whole's. Annual and quarters are compared where the declaration names them | a segment quarter above the company quarter |\n    74\t| **C4 one forecast per shared quantity** [46b.1, 46c.1] | for each shared quantity and period the declaration names, each scenario has exactly one `basis: forecast` node. **Every path** in the graph from a member's outcome to any figure that measures that quantity and period passes through that node. A guide, consensus or other source of a future period may feed the forecast node; no path from an outcome may reach it while avoiding the node | the $108B guide feeding v14's $113.5B forecast and, on another path, v9's Data Center Q3 directly: the same guide node, a bypass |\n    75\t| **C5 reference** [46b.3, 46c.1] | each answer's reference is `unavailable` with a reason, or a direct `source` figure of basis consensus, guidance or third_party from a passage whose speaker is not the system, or a `computed` figure whose whole graph is computed figures over such sources and `reported` sources (q02: $392B consensus × 92% reported mix). No `estimate`, `forecast` or `prior_forecast` anywhere in the reference's graph. Its provenance is shown | the system's own forecast presented as the market's, directly or wrapped in an estimate that cites outside evidence |\n    76\t| **C6 rung and outcome** [46.2] | the chosen rung's band overlaps the outcome (a point: contains it). The reasons say why that band, where the range overlaps several | a rung no part of the outcome falls in |\n    77\t| **C7 structure** [46.5] | exactly one answer per member; an answer's outcome `what` matches its member's question (population, measure, scope, period, from the question's typed key); figure ids are unique; references are not dangling and not circular; operations come from the closed set | a missing or doubled member, a borrowed outcome, a dangling or circular figure |\n    78\t| **C8 operand compatibility** [46b.3] | by operation. **sum/difference:** same measure, population and scope, with periods either disjoint and together forming the output period (`time_aggregation`), or identical with a declared relation (`accounting_parts` needs a declared `part_of`). **roll_forward:** an opening balance as of a date, and flows over a period starting then or later, give the closing balance [46c.2]. **per unit** [gate run 1]: a total over a price per unit gives units, and units times that price give the total, for one population and scope (q19's $20B at $5,000 a Vera CPU is four million units). **share:** numerator and denominator stated, with a declared `part_of` or a reported mix. **product/ratio:** a change of measure, population, scope or period not covered above needs a bridge estimate whose `converts` names the actual operands, and an assumption | 730/800 × $1.3T with no bridge naming e1786's four-company 2026 spending and e2535's five-company capex; a bridge that converts other operands |\n    79\t\n    80\t| **C4 scenarios** [46g.3, 46h.1, 46i.1] | each scenario has one forecast node per shared quantity. Scenario membership is derived along the whole path: no figure of a point scenario may depend, at any depth, on a forecast node of another scenario. A `range` summary is an estimate with low and high, is an answer's outcome, and feeds no figure; only it may rest on several scenarios; a bypass is a path that reaches the quantity through no forecast node of any scenario | a low path on the high total; a guide relabelled as an alternate scenario and read by the base |\n    81\t| **C4 derived scenarios** [46j.1] | a figure's scenarios are those of the estimates it rests on at any depth; a point-scenario figure may rest on no estimate of another scenario; related members (`part_of`) cover the same scenarios, so C3 compares like with like | the company in `base` and Data Center in `alt`; crossed share estimates |\n    82\t| **C5 one choice** [46g.2] | a reference names exactly one of a figure or `unavailable` | a prior figure hidden beside an \"unavailable\" reason |\n    83\t| **C9 same base** [46f.3, 46j.2] | for each `same_base(later, earlier)`, the later member rests on the earlier member's forecast nodes (its outcome, or the scenario paths behind its range summary), and every path to a figure of the earlier quantity and period passes through one of them; scenarios match by C4 | FY2028 grown from the $392B consensus instead of q01's $400–435B forecast |\n    84\t| **C10 earlier forecasts** [46f.3, 46f.4, 46h.2, 46i.2] | a figure is an earlier forecast when its kind is prior, its basis is prior_forecast or it carries a pointer (derived, not labelled), and it must then be kind prior with basis prior_forecast; such a figure points to an earlier answer shown in the request and equals a figure that answer states; it feeds no figure and is no answer's outcome | an invented or misdated earlier figure; an earlier forecast used as an input |\n    85\t| **C2 bound direction** [46f.3] | for positive operands, a result's bound side follows its inputs: sums and products keep a lower or upper side, a ratio's or difference's second operand flips it, and mixed sides give `approx` | \"more than double\" times $20B declared below $40B |\n    86\t\n    87\t**Recorded, not refused** (visible to readers and to the page) [46.3]:\n    88\t- **Overlap:** a `sum` or `roll_forward` with relation `overlap_unknown`. Its alternate recomputes that same calculation without the\n    89\t  named `disputed` operand, all else fixed [46b.4, 46d].\n    90\t- **Adjustment:** an `estimate` that `adjusts` a figure. Its alternate is the adjusted figure (R4: the bound \">40\").\n    91\t- **Bridge:** every bridge; alternate undetermined.\n    92\t- **Boundaries** [46f.3]: a point alternate, or a bound lying beyond the outcome on its own side, decides a crossing definitely; a bound on\n    93\t  the near side only possibly. Code recomputes, from unrounded values, each outcome under each defined alternate. It records which rung boundary the\n    94\t  assumption definitely or possibly decides: \"crosses $40B\" when the alternate is a bound, a rung change when the alternate is a\n    95\t  point. Where a downstream estimate has no formula, or no alternate is defined, it records \"undetermined\". A reader decides whether a\n    96\t  boundary-deciding assumption is supported. D721/D722: unresolved inclusion is never treated as proof of double counting.\n    97\t\n    98\t**The model keeps:** drivers, weights, discounts, scenarios, the choice among overlapping bands, and whether to step off the\n    99\treference. Code never prefers guidance, prescribes a haircut, refuses an estimate for being one, or picks a band from a midpoint.\n   100\t\n   101\t## 4. Tables\n   102\t\n   103\t**Value × reader.**\n   104\t- **The calculation, the recorded assumptions and band-deciding flags:** read by the checks, the readers' packets, and later the\n   105\t  page.\n   106\t- **`unresolved`:** read by the round (no member updated) and the bundle (\"Forecast unavailable\", D726).\n   107\t- **The family declaration:** read by the request builder, C3, C4 and C7.\n   108\t- **Nothing in poc-v118 reads any of it.**\n   109\t\n   110\t**New path × old checks.**\n   111\t- v7's id, push and theme checks apply per member answer. Rung checks apply to `forecast` answers only [46b.5]. `cites` names shown\n   112\t  evidence only.\n   113\t- `cites_views` is always empty.\n   114\t- v7's request bytes are unchanged (the frozen pins).\n   115\t\n   116\t**State × event (the test harness).** Inputs are fixed: the stored requests' passages and the gold packet's items, as of the stored\n   117\tday.\n   118\t\n   119\t| Event | Outcome |\n   120\t|---|---|\n   121\t| success | the calculation and answers are recorded with their check results |\n   122\t| refusal | the errors are recorded; one retry with them |\n   123\t| second refusal | the family is unresolved, recorded |\n   124\t| a member's own `unresolved` [46b.5] | recorded; the family is held (no member updated) when any other member's outcome depends on a shared quantity or relation involving it; otherwise the others publish if they pass |\n   125\t| crash midway | the attempt is recorded incomplete and counts as no result |\n   126\t\n   127\tNothing writes a product store. Production lifecycle (members closing or opening, families changing) is designed only if the test\n   128\tpasses.\n   129\t\n   130\t**Prior decisions:**\n   131\t- D24, D28: bounded to the figures open questions consume.\n   132\t- D198: evidence in view, unioned.\n   133\t- D260, D263: the standard kept.\n   134\t- D359, D723: no product launcher.\n   135\t- D695: number questions.\n   136\t- D711, D721, D722: linking passed conclusions, and unresolved inclusion is not double counting.\n   137\t- D725, D726.\n   138\t\n   139\t## 5. The v13 request text (written and audited at $0 before the gate closes) [46.6]\n   140\t\n   141\t- **The basis:** judging-v7's standard sentences are kept, among them the reference, run-rate, discount, sizing, identities and\n   142\t  consistency.\n   143\t- **Replaced where they conflict with a family:**\n   144\t  - the single-question delivery (\"the current conclusions of dependencies\");\n   145\t  - \"the previous conclusion carries\" pieces not shown, which becomes \"our earlier forecast, not a source\";\n   146\t  - the one-reference, one-outcome output sentence;\n   147\t  - the dependency-consistency sentence, which becomes the family's own consistency, the same clause made family-wide.\n   148\t- **Added:** how to write figures (kinds, bases, expressions, relations, bridges, `adjusts`, `unresolved`).\n   149\t- **The audit:** every changed sentence is shown with its source (D260, D263, D725, this design), read against a stored family\n   150\t  request, and its character count before and after is reported (AGENTS §6).\n   151\t\n   152\t## 6. The $0 gate (registered; each defect must produce its named finding) [46.5]\n   153\t\n   154\t| Item | Content | Pass: the named finding |\n   155\t|---|---|---|\n   156\t| R1a | old-2 8729 (v14) and 8737 (v9) on 09-20, as stored: v9 Q4 146, v14 Q4 127 | **C3** on Q4 |\n   157\t| R1b | the same, with Q3 as stored: **one** guide node (midpoint 108 from e2499, labelled `guidance`) feeds v14's company Q3 forecast (113.5, beat-adjusted) and, on a second path, v9's Data Center Q3 (108 × 92.5% = 99.9) | **C4** on Q3 company revenue (the bypass path), independent of C3 |\n   158\t| R1c (control) | the same guide node (midpoint 108 ±2%, e2499's expression) feeding one shared Q3 forecast node (113.5) that both members read | passes |\n   159\t| R2 | old-2 8734 as `roll_forward`: the $25B balance as of July 26 (e2620), plus the $18B committed for the rest of the fiscal year (e2889) as `overlap_unknown` and `disputed`, plus MediaTek and the talks as estimates | passes C8; recorded overlap; the alternate without the $18B (about $35B) decides the $50B boundary (conditional) |\n   160\t| R3 | old-2 8595: 730 (e1786, bound \">\") / 800 (e2535, bound \"approx_below\") × 1,300 (e2535, point) | stripped qualifiers: **C1**. Qualifiers kept: passes C1, then **C8** without a bridge naming those operands. With the bridge: recorded, undetermined |\n   161\t| R4 | old-3 9166: 20 × (>2) (e3660, \"more than double\") gives >40, trimmed to 38–39 by `adjusts: trim` | recorded adjustment that definitely crosses the $40B boundary (rung 2 is $40–50B, rung 1 $50B+) |\n   162\t| G1–G4 (controls) | q01, q02, q19, q22, from `gold-v1.json`'s reasoning (expressions from the packet items). q01's Q1 + Q2 as `time_aggregation`; q02's comparison $392B (consensus) × 92% (reported mix); q22 in one family with q01, reading q01's FY2027 node | all pass, estimates visible; q02's reference passes C5; q19's overlapping range passes C6 with its reason |\n   163\t| P1–P3 (plants, on control copies) | P1, an amount absent from its expression; P2, a wrong sum; P3a, a prior forecast as the reference; P3b, the same figure wrapped in an estimate that cites outside evidence | P1 **C1**, P2 **C2**, P3a and P3b **C5**, each for its intended reason |\n   164\t| T1–T7 (C1 forms, real passages) | e2499 midpoint 108 ±2% (accepted as `midpoint`); e1786 \">730\"; e2535 approx_below 800 and the point 1,300; e3660 \">×2\"; stripped forms \"$730\", \"800\", \"double\" against those passages; 108.5 against e2499 | T1–T3 parse as stated; the stripped forms and 108.5 refused |\n   165\t| U1–U2 (paths) | a member answering `unresolved` while another member's outcome needs its shared quantity; a calculation refused twice | the family held, recorded; the family unresolved, recorded |\n   166\t\n   167\t- **Stop:** any missing finding, a finding for the wrong reason, or a control the format cannot express.\n   168\t- **Then:** Council B on the gate's output. The paid gate's ceiling goes to Alex.\n     1\t# judging-v13 paid gate: plan, harness and reading rule (registered before any send; D725, D729, D730)\n     2\t\n     3\t## 1. What is sent\n     4\t\n     5\t`paid_gate.py plan paid-plan.json` builds 12 requests from the stored runs.\n     6\t\n     7\t| Unit | Arm | Members | Evidence shown |\n     8\t|---|---|---|---|\n     9\t| pair-old-1 (09-20: v4 total, v8 Data Center) | joint; separate-v4; separate-v8 | both; one; one | the union of both stored requests' evidence in view (237 items), in every arm |\n    10\t| pair-old-2 (v14, v9) | joint; separate-v14; separate-v9 | as above | 237 |\n    11\t| pair-old-3 (v3, v35) | joint; separate-v3; separate-v35 | as above | 260 |\n    12\t| single-O (old-2 v29, equity commitments) | single | v29 | its own request's (35) |\n    13\t| single-S (old-2 v6, four hyperscalers' 2027 capex) | single | v6 | 31 |\n    14\t| single-E (old-3 v41, FY2028 Vera CPU) | single | v41 | 15 |\n    15\t\n    16\t- **Each request:** judging-v13's system text (`judging-v13-draft.txt`, 4,373 characters); the family header with the exact outcome\n    17\t  keys; the evidence; the themes once. No earlier forecast is shown (D728). The strict v13 schema.\n    18\t- **The model:** poc-v118's re-judging setting (gpt-6-luna, medium effort, 16,000 output tokens).\n    19\t- **Draws:** three per request, so 36 first attempts.\n    20\t- **Retry:** a calculation the checks refuse, or a schema-invalid answer, is retried once with the reasons. A second refusal makes the\n    21\t  unit unresolved.\n    22\t- **What is scored:** the first attempt, on both arms (§3f). Retries are sent and recorded for diagnosis only.\n    23\t- **The separate arm:** its two first answers per pair and draw are merged and checked over the pair's family (C3, C4 across the two).\n    24\t\n    25\t## 2. Spend and stops\n    26\t\n    27\t- **The ledger:** one, at Alex's ceiling (`paid_gate.py send <folder> <ceiling> paid-plan.json` refuses any other ceiling).\n    28\t- **Budget:** the real budget transport and stop gate, which reserve each request's worst case before sending. After a stop, nothing\n    29\t  more is sent, and the remaining units are recorded as not sent.\n    30\t- **Estimate** (at `hivemind.model.PRICES`: gpt-6-luna, $0.10 input and $0.50 output per million tokens; tokens estimated as\n    31\t  characters / 4):\n    32\t  - a pair request is about 34–38k input tokens and a singleton 6–9k;\n    33\t  - the 36 first attempts cost about $0.17 at 4,000 output tokens each, and at most $0.39 if every answer used its 16,000-token cap;\n    34\t  - the transport reserves at most $0.039 for one request.\n    35\t  - **Proposed ceiling: $0.50.** It covers every scored attempt. Retries come last (§3g), so a ceiling stop among them leaves the\n    36\t    scored test complete.\n    37\t- **Sends:** one at a time, every first attempt before any retry (§3g). Each send has its own archive, so no draw is answered from\n    38\t  another.\n    39\t\n    40\t## 3. The $0 rehearsal (`paid_rehearsal.py`, `rehearsal.log`: 16 of 16)\n    41\t\n    42\t- **Clean run:** 36 first attempts, every HTTP body byte-equal to an independently built request, every unit published, the separate\n    43\t  arm reconciled in 9 of 9, the spend settled under the ceiling.\n    44\t- **Paths:**\n    45\t  - a refused calculation is retried, then published;\n    46\t  - two refusals give unresolved;\n    47\t  - a schema-invalid answer is retried;\n    48\t  - two separate answers that each pass alone are refused when reconciled (C3);\n    49\t  - after a ledger stop, nothing more is sent.\n    50\t- **Packets:** 27 packets with the arm hidden. The verdict is computed on synthetic readings: both arms good gives STOP (gain under 3);\n    51\t  a joint gain of 4 gives PASS; one published singleton defect gives STOP; a separate-arm defect alone does not stop; a missing\n    52\t  reading gives INCOMPLETE.\n    53\t\n    54\t## 3b. After council round 47 (`codex-r47.md`)\n    55\t\n    56\t- **[47.1] Reconciliation:** forecasts of the same quantity and scenario with equal recomputed values and bounds are unified before the\n    57\t  family's checks run. The duplicate's derivation is kept in results. Forecasts that differ stay as a conflict. Rehearsed: an agreeing\n    58\t  pair passes, and a disagreeing pair is refused (C4).\n    59\t- **[47.2] One authorization:**\n    60\t  - `paid-plan.sha256` pins the reviewed plan, and the sender refuses any other plan;\n    61\t  - `~/.hivemind-keys/v13-paid-gate/authorization.json` is created exclusively at the first send, so a second launch is refused;\n    62\t  - (unit, arm) pairs are unique.\n    63\t- **[47.3] Every attempt recorded:**\n    64\t  - a `started` line is written before the provider is called;\n    65\t  - checker failures and arithmetic errors are refusals, recorded with the raw answer;\n    66\t  - every planned unit has a final status (`finals.json`), even after an abort;\n    67\t  - a refused reservation never reaches the provider.\n    68\t- **[47.4] Readings validated:** exact packets and cards, two readings (A, B) per card with yes or no and a ground for each question,\n    69\t  and a third reading on every disagreement. Otherwise the verdict is INCOMPLETE.\n    70\t- **[47.5] Packets:**\n    71\t  - the question text and ladder;\n    72\t  - each passage exactly as the request showed it, including those named only in `rests_on`;\n    73\t  - declared figures with renumbered ids, the recomputed outcomes and the recorded assumptions;\n    74\t  - the cards a non-reconciled separate unit has;\n    75\t  - neutral decision labels.\n    76\t- **Rehearsal:** 27 of 27 (`rehearsal.log`). The $0 gate still passes (`gate-run27.txt`: 87 of 87), and the mutants are unchanged.\n    77\t\n    78\t## 3c. After council round 47b (`codex-r47b.md`)\n    79\t\n    80\t- **[47b.1] Reconciliation:**\n    81\t  - Two recomputed values are the same when their bounds match and each end agrees within 1e-9 relative. A floating-point copy\n    82\t    unifies; a real difference stays a conflict.\n    83\t  - The readers never see the unified calculation. Each card of a reconciled packet is shown with its own original answer.\n    84\t- **[47b.2] Completeness comes from the attempt records.** `answers.jsonl` is the one record of what happened. For each planned unit\n    85\t  and draw, the final status is derived from it:\n    86\t  - no started line: **not sent**;\n    87\t  - a started line with no finished line: **interrupted**;\n    88\t  - a first refusal with no retry sent: **retry not sent**;\n    89\t  - otherwise, the last attempt's decision, and a second refusal is **unresolved**.\n    90\t\n    91\t  `results.json` is written from these records in a `finally`, so an abort still leaves it. The verdict is INCOMPLETE for every\n    92\t  status other than published, held or unresolved. That includes a reservation or HTTP failure, recorded as **transport**.\n    93\t- **[47b.3] Packets:**\n    94\t  - Every attempt of every card is listed, with neutral labels and check codes only.\n    95\t  - Each card is its own derivation: the figures its outcome and reference rest on, renumbered F1.. for that card alone.\n    96\t  - Its recorded assumptions are mapped to the same numbers. A record that names a figure outside the card is left out.\n    97\t- **[47b.4] Binding:**\n    98\t  - `packets.md` and the key are each created exclusively, once. A second `packets` call is refused.\n    99\t  - The key carries the run's digest (`answers.jsonl`, `results.json`, `plan.json`) and the sha256 of `packets.md`.\n   100\t  - The readings file carries the sha256 of the packets it was read on.\n   101\t  - The verdict is INCOMPLETE when any of the three differs, or when a ground is not a non-empty string naming a passage (e…) or a\n   102\t    figure (F…) of that packet.\n   103\t- **Rehearsal:** 41 of 41 (`rehearsal.log`). The new cases:\n   104\t  - a floating-point copy unifies;\n   105\t  - a reconciled card shows its own derivation;\n   106\t  - every attempt is listed;\n   107\t  - a transport failure makes the verdict INCOMPLETE;\n   108\t  - an interruption during a retry leaves results, marks the unit interrupted and the rest not sent;\n   109\t  - a retry that was never sent is incomplete;\n   110\t  - the card view maps assumption records and leaves out other cards';\n   111\t  - a second packets run, readings made on other packets, a run changed after freezing, and null or foreign grounds are each refused\n   112\t    or INCOMPLETE.\n   113\t- **Mutants:** 13 of 13 caught, and the control passes (`paid_mutants.py`, `paid-mutants.log`). Each mutant breaks one of these\n   114\t  defences on a scratch copy. The plan is unchanged (sha256 `6f81dae5…`), and `calc_check.py` is unchanged since gate run 27.\n   115\t\n   116\t## 3d. After council round 47c (`codex-r47c.md`)\n   117\t\n   118\t- **[47c.1] Reconciliation, by the joint arm's rule:**\n   119\t  - Only a forecast of a declared shared quantity is unified, and only with the other call's forecast of the same quantity and\n   120\t    scenario when their values are the same.\n   121\t  - Duplicates inside one call are never unified, so C3 and C4 judge them as they judge a joint answer.\n   122\t- **[47c.2] The key only orders the packets.**\n   123\t  - Each packet's decision, cards and allowed grounds come from the run.\n   124\t  - The verdict renders all packets again from the run and the key's order, and requires the text to equal `packets.md`, the key's\n   125\t    sha256 and the readings' sha256.\n   126\t  - The key must map exactly one packet to each unit of the run. A decision written into the key is ignored.\n   127\t- **[47c.3] Abstentions and attempts:**\n   128\t  - Each card is labelled K1, K2, … and is itself a valid ground, so a no-figure abstention can be read.\n   129\t  - Every earlier attempt shows its own answer, with figures named T<n>.F…. An attempt with no parsed answer shows its recorded\n   130\t    failure as a neutral label.\n   131\t  - Check codes are not shown: a cross-member code (C3, or C4 across members) occurs only in a joint answer and would reveal the arm.\n   132\t    The reasons stay in `results.json`.\n   133\t- **[47c.4] A torn journal:**\n   134\t  - A line that does not parse is skipped and listed as damaged. Its attempt counts as unfinished.\n   135\t  - `paid_gate.py recover <folder>` rebuilds `results.json` read-only and sends nothing.\n   136\t  - The verdict is INCOMPLETE while any line is damaged.\n   137\t- **Rehearsal:** 48 of 48, three times (`rehearsal.log`). The new cases:\n   138\t  - two equal forecasts inside one answer, and two equal shared forecasts inside one call, are both refused after reconciliation, as\n   139\t    in a joint answer;\n   140\t  - an abstention reads with K1 as its ground;\n   141\t  - earlier attempts show their answers, with no codes;\n   142\t  - a key that maps packets to other units is INCOMPLETE;\n   143\t  - a decision written into the key changes nothing;\n   144\t  - a torn journal recovers with 35 units published and the torn one interrupted, and its verdict is INCOMPLETE.\n   145\t- **A rehearsal fault found and traced:** one parallel control run failed 47 of 48.\n   146\t  - The swap test exchanged P01 and P02. In about 2 of 26 shuffles, these are two draws of the same unit and arm.\n   147\t  - The rehearsal's draws answer alike, so their text is identical and the swap changes nothing.\n   148\t  - That is harmless to the verdict, which counts by unit, not by draw. The test now swaps two different units.\n   149\t- **Mutants:** 19 of 19 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`).\n   150\t  - Three missed in the first run and were traced:\n   151\t    - one was stale, because packets now render from the attempts;\n   152\t    - one was masked by the shared-only filter, and a shared within-call case was added;\n   153\t    - one disabled only half a condition.\n   154\t  - One mutant is equivalent and is left out: unifying any key across calls, not only declared shared ones. C3 looks only at\n   155\t    forecasts reachable from each member's own outcome, so the result does not change.\n   156\t\n   157\t## 3e. After council round 47d (`codex-r47d.md`): three structural rules\n   158\t\n   159\tRounds 47b to 47d kept finding the same two things: process information inside the readers' packets, and a mapping from packets to\n   160\tunits that could change after reading. These findings were answered by rule, not one by one.\n   161\t\n   162\t1. **The scoring text holds only what the four questions need.** For each card: the question, the ladder, the passages it names,\n   163\t   its own figures, the recomputed outcome, the recorded assumptions and the card. It carries no attempt history, no code decision\n   164\t   and no check code. The verdict applies the code's decision itself.\n   165\t   - Earlier attempts go to `attempts.md`. That file is for diagnostic reading only and is never scored. Each attempt sits under its\n   166\t     own shuffled label (D01, …), with no link to a packet. [47d.3]\n   167\t   - A card with no final answer says why in plain words: no request was sent; the request was started and no answer was recorded;\n   168\t     no answer arrived; the answer was refused and its retry was not sent; or the answer did not match the format. [47d.4]\n   169\t2. **The text the readers receive commits to the mapping.** The key is {a random salt, the run's digest, the packet order, the\n   170\t   attempt labels}. Its sha256 is the first line of `packets.md`. The verdict refuses a key whose sha256 differs from that line. It\n   171\t   also renders the packets again from the run and the key, and refuses a mismatch with `packets.md` or with the readings' sha256.\n   172\t   A swap of two packets with identical text is caught, and so is a decision written into the key. [47d.1]\n   173\t3. **Reconciliation is one to one.** A declared shared forecast is unified only when each of the two calls holds exactly one\n   174\t   candidate for that quantity and scenario, and their values are the same. Otherwise every copy stays, for C3 and C4 to judge as in a\n   175\t   joint answer. [47d.2]\n   176\t\n   177\t- **The record:** an attempt that was started and not finished is kept in `results.json` as interrupted.\n   178\t- **Rehearsal:** 51 of 51, three times (`rehearsal.log`). The new cases:\n   179\t  - the scoring text has no attempt, decision or code;\n   180\t  - the refused first answer appears only in `attempts.md`;\n   181\t  - a swap of identical-text packets is INCOMPLETE;\n   182\t  - a forged key is INCOMPLETE;\n   183\t  - two equal shared forecasts in the second call are refused (C4, three nodes);\n   184\t  - an interrupted card says the request was started.\n   185\t- **Mutants:** 20 of 20 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`).\n   186\t\n   187\t## 3f. After council round 47e (`codex-r47e.md`)\n   188\t\n   189\t- **[47e.1] The first attempt is scored, on both arms.**\n   190\t  - In the joint arm, a cross-card conflict (C3) is refused and retried with its reason, so the retry can repair it. In the separate\n   191\t    arm, the same conflict appears only when the two answers are reconciled, after both calls are over, and there is no retry.\n   192\t  - Scoring the final answers would credit the joint arm with a second chance the separate arm cannot have.\n   193\t  - D725 registered both arms \"under the same format and checks\" and says nothing about retries. Its pass and stop lines stay as\n   194\t    written and are applied to the first attempts.\n   195\t  - Retries are still sent and read for diagnosis (`attempts.md`), and are not counted.\n   196\t  - Rehearsed: the joint pair is published after its retry, but its refused first attempt is scored, so both arms come out at 6.\n   197\t- **[47e.2] One unit.** A shared forecast is unified only when the two copies have the same unit, as well as the same value and bounds.\n   198\t  Rehearsed: 110 USD bn against 110 USD is refused (C4).\n   199\t- **[47e.3] Every returned text is readable.**\n   200\t  - When the format check fails an answer, the scoring packet keeps its neutral label.\n   201\t  - The text the model returned, and the passages it names, go to `attempts.md` under a blind label. This covers both a first attempt\n   202\t    and a final one that fails the format.\n   203\t- **[47e.4] One verdict per run.**\n   204\t  - `verdict` copies the readings into the run once (`readings.json`, created exclusively).\n   205\t  - It scores them and writes `verdict.json` once, holding the readings' sha256, the key commitment, the packets' sha256, the run digest\n   206\t    and the result.\n   207\t  - A second verdict on the run is refused. `paid_gate.py replay` recomputes from the sealed readings and must reproduce the result.\n   208\t  - Rehearsed: the second verdict is refused; replay reproduces the result; after the sealed readings are edited, replay fails.\n   209\t- **Rehearsal:** 55 of 55, run three times (`rehearsal.log`).\n   210\t- **Mutants:** 25 of 25 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`).\n   211\t  - Two obsolete mutants were repointed or removed.\n   212\t  - One mutant crashed on a missing file and not on the defence. The test was corrected, and the mutant now fails the right check.\n   213\t\n   214\t## 3g. After council round 47f (`codex-r47f.md`)\n   215\t\n   216\t- **[47f.1] Retries cannot decide whether the scored test is complete.**\n   217\t  - The run sends every first attempt (all 36) before any retry.\n   218\t  - Completeness is judged on the first attempts: each must end published, held or refused.\n   219\t  - A retry that fails in transport, is interrupted or is never sent stays in the diagnostic record.\n   220\t  - Rehearsed: an interruption during a retry leaves all 36 scored attempts and a complete verdict. A transport failure on a retry\n   221\t    leaves the verdict complete. An interruption during a scored attempt makes the verdict INCOMPLETE. In the journal, every first\n   222\t    attempt comes before every retry.\n   223\t- **[47f.2] Every returned text survives.**\n   224\t  - The record keeps the provider's whole response, as the archive stores it, with no 20,000-character cut.\n   225\t  - A null content is shown as the model's refusal, or as \"no text returned\".\n   226\t  - Both files are rendered before anything is written, and a failed render leaves no files.\n   227\t  - Rehearsed: a refusal with null content, a 25,000-character malformed answer whose passage is named at its end, and a render that\n   228\t    fails and then succeeds.\n   229\t- **Rehearsal:** 61 of 61, three runs (`rehearsal.log`).\n   230\t- **Mutants:** 30 of 30 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`). Two mutants are caught by the crash the defence prevents:\n   231\t  - without the interrupted branch, the summary fails on an unfinished attempt;\n   232\t  - passing a null content on, packet rendering fails.\n   233\t\n   234\t## 3h. After council round 47g (`codex-r47g.md`)\n   235\t\n   236\t- **[47g] Two journals, read on the writer's own delimiter.**\n   237\t  - Scored first attempts are written to `answers.jsonl`, and diagnostic retries to `retries.jsonl`. The run digest covers both.\n   238\t  - A damaged line in `answers.jsonl` makes the verdict INCOMPLETE. A damaged line in `retries.jsonl` is reported and marks that retry\n   239\t    interrupted, but leaves the scored test complete.\n   240\t  - Records are split on '\\n' only, the writer's delimiter. An answer may contain other Unicode line separators (U+2028, U+0085,\n   241\t    U+2029), and these are kept inside the record.\n   242\t  - Rehearsed: a torn retry record leaves the verdict complete; a torn scored record (the existing control) makes it INCOMPLETE; an\n   243\t    answer holding U+2028, U+0085 and U+2029 stays one record; the order of every first attempt before any retry is checked on the\n   244\t    provider's own call sequence.\n   245\t- **Rehearsal:** 63 of 63, three runs (`rehearsal.log`).\n   246\t- **Mutants:** 33 of 33 caught, and the control passes (`paid-mutants.log`).\n   247\t  - One mutant (splitting on any line break) is caught when a scenario guard stops the rehearsal: 36 attempts started, 35 finished.\n   248\t  - The mutant harness now counts such a stop as a catch and shows its reason.\n   249\t\n   250\t## 4. The reading rule (fixed now, before any answer exists)\n   251\t\n   252\t- **Packets:** 27 (`paid_gate.py packets`): 9 joint pair-draws, 9 reconciled separate pair-draws and 9 singleton draws. The labels are\n   253\t  shuffled, the arm is hidden, and the key is kept in `~/.hivemind-keys/v13-paid-gate/`.\n   254\t  - Each packet shows, per card (K1, K2), the question, the ladder, every passage the final answer names (as the request showed it),\n   255\t    its figures, the recomputed outcome, the recorded assumptions and the card. Nothing about attempts or the code's decision is\n   256\t    shown (§3e).\n   257\t  - A packet the code did not publish (held, unresolved, not reconciled) is read too. The verdict, not the reader, applies the code's\n   258\t    decision: its cards cannot count as usable.\n   259\t  - The readers receive `packets.md` exactly, with its key-commitment line. The readings file carries that text's sha256.\n   260\t- **Readers:** two fresh readers of different families, a Codex session and a Claude subagent, each blind to the other and to the arm.\n   261\t  For each card each answers four questions, yes or no, with the passage that decides:\n   262\t  1. **usable:** the outcome is usable and supported by the shown passages it rests on;\n   263\t  2. **contradiction:** with the other card of its packet, it states a hard numerical contradiction;\n   264\t  3. **internal_reference:** the system's own forecast is presented as the market's expectation;\n   265\t  4. **deciding_gap:** an unresolved overlap, a change of scope or an unsized adjustment decides its band.\n   266\t- **Agreement:** where the two readers agree, that is the reading. Where they disagree, a third fresh reader (Codex) reads the card with\n   267\t  both readings and the passages, and its answer decides. No reading is changed after the verdict is computed.\n   268\t- **Verdict** (`paid_gate.py verdict`, D725's lines, on the first attempts; computed once and sealed, §3f):\n   269\t  - **PASS:** the joint arm has both cards usable in at least 8 of 9 pairs, at least 2 of 3 per snapshot, and at least 3 more usable\n   270\t    pairs than the separate arm; and zero published defects (contradiction, internal reference or deciding gap) across the joint and\n   271\t    singleton answers.\n   272\t  - **STOP:** any such published defect; a coverage line missed; a gain under 3.\n   273\t  - **INCOMPLETE:** a packet or a card missing a reading, or a unit not sent.\n   274\t  - This is a small design test, not release acceptance.\n   275\t\n   276\t## 5. What the result decides\n   277\t\n   278\t- **PASS:** judging-v13 earns a production design: automatic families in matching, lifecycle and the request builder, each a further\n   279\t  step Alex opens.\n   280\t- **STOP or INCOMPLETE:** the line goes back to Alex with the readings, and nothing is tuned on these 36 answers.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^##|''^###|a field.s definition|Before a version is rejected|Check before the send'\"' AGENTS.md; rg -n '\"'^###? D(690|725|731|732|733)'\"\\\\b' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; tail -n 9 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^##|''^###|a field.s definition|Before a version is rejected|Check before the send'\"' AGENTS.md; rg -n '\"'^###? D(690|725|731|732|733)'\"\\\\b' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; tail -n 9 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md\"","aggregated_output":"9:## Where to look first\n27:## Authority and scope\n59:## 1. Before acting on a failure: diagnose\n91:## 2. Before writing code: design\n122:## 3. Writing code\n145:## 4. Tests\n168:## 5. Verification\n194:## 6. Writing prompts\n231:- **Check before the send, at no cost.** Write one answer that follows the prompt and run every code check on it: a request never asks\n237:## 7. Measuring and iterating\n239:### 7.1 The ruler\n292:### 7.2 Reading results\n326:### 7.3 Iterating\n373:  another class, the call is doing several jobs: move the rule to its owner (a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field)\n380:- **Before a version is rejected, the rejection is checked.** Every failed or refused answer behind a \"did not pass\" is read against its\n388:## 8. Product thinking\n419:## 9. Workspace and operations\n449:## 10. Rebuilding from ../tn-hivemind\n456:## 11. Design documents and decisions\n485:## Before finishing a slice\n10360:## D690 · 2026-11-20 · Alex chooses A: poc-v118 is the product, the research line rests until his new direction; the D681–D689 records are committed locally\n10957:## D725 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 45/45b: the number answers fail because related numbers have no shared, checked calculation; one judge version and one test recommended to Alex\n11083:## D731 · 2026-11-22 · judging-v13's paid-gate harness is ready to send (council round 47h); the first attempt is scored on both arms; the ceiling is Alex's\n11110:## D732 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: the paid gate's ceiling is $0.50\n| 33 | 11-05 | Alex：\"改\"。只改读法里那一句：原句\"写出组成它的行、或报告记它的那一行\"→ 新句\"只写算出它的那几行，或换了名字的同一个量；更大的行、旁边的行都不写\"（任务 790 → 889 字符，理由：抽象说法换成闭合两类加边界）。提示词审计、Codex 只读审 1 轮（1 条严重：发送失败会被当成白卷，已改成\"不完整、不下结论\"）；先在 v19、v20、v10、v12 上试发 24 次（1.6 美分） | 试发过：v19、v20 三次都白卷，v10 3/3；v12 2/3——问模型（2 次）：新句的\"更大的行\"半句让它把\"购置固定资产和无形资产\"当成比资本开支宽的一行，其中一次重想后改口\"就是资本开支\" | 照登记测全部 22 道题 |\n| 34 | 11-05 | 第三版的登记测试：和第 8 轮一样，只换这一句；读 132 次（0.078 美元）、两人分类（第二位盲分）、读到数的 7 道题三遍重判、当天对照（108 次，0.268 美元） | **三条全过**：v10、v12 各 9/9；判对的题一道没变错（只有 v22 第 2 遍反过来对照 0/3、起点组 3/3，理由是在 41% 和 32% 两个增长估计间摇摆，和第 13 轮方向相反，与起点数无关）；46 个数全是自己的量（2 条是标明的二季度指引），两人分类全一致；v19、v20 的错数全没了，其余 15 道白卷、没冒出新错。没盖住：资本开支那一行试发时漏过 1 次；指引也被写成\"事实\"；一路判下来的效果和跨题效果要端到端才量得到 | 交 Alex：要不要做进生产、跑 14 天三遍端到端 |\n| 35 | 11-05 | 独立读者挑战\"现在做进生产、跑 14 天三遍\"的建议 | 核实属实的：资本开支那一行在新句子下 12 次读漏 3 次（旧句子 9/9，模型两次都怪\"更大的行\"半句）；句子照 5 月财报改、又只在 5 月财报上量，没有留出的财报；做进生产前的记录写法（同一个数挂几道题、指引不该写成\"事实\"、第二个提示词）要 Alex 定；分类和判断同时跑、说明里还引着旧句子，照实记下 | 建议撤回；下一步：修掉资本开支漏读（改成\"写完逐条复核\"），在 5 月和留出的 8 月两份财报上各测一遍，约 0.5 美元 |\n| 36 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好开始做到production 上，完整的更新所有系统设计等\"；同一个数挂几道题，他选\"存一条，挂到每道读到它的题\"（D364）。做进生产前先修资本开支漏读：第四版把\"是不是这道题的量\"挪到最后逐条复核（并删掉别的期间），文件规则补\"有申报的只读申报的\"；Codex 审 3 轮（共 6 条严重，都改）；试发 7 道题过；然后 5 月登记读法、8 月留出读法 | **没过**：5 月\"不能删掉该留的\"——v22 只留住 1/3、资本开支 3/6 读；8 月资本开支二季度 0/3（选了上半年、引用跳格被拒）。机理：复核里\"别的期间删掉\"让全年题删掉最近一季；\"最近一期\"在本季和年初至今两栏间没说清。好消息：8 月 v9 找到了新披露的担保 1,085 亿和承诺 560 亿 | 停，交 Alex：第三版是唯一过了 5 月三条的版本 |\n| 37 | 11-05 | Alex：\"为什么没过？…你确定是正确的rejection 吗？你又下去看trace吗？\"；\"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信下一步你要跑什么才能收敛\"。普查全部 7 次读法、606 次文件读（`census_all_reads.py`，$0） | 第三版 0 个错数、156 次读漏 3 次（全是资本开支）；第二版 2 个错数；第四版漏 24 次（5 月）、16 次（8 月）。我自己判错的：6 个对的数被我的引用检查拒（漏星号、跳格），8 月\"只认本季\"规则错，第 13 轮 3 条\"有争议\"的数是标准答案自己用的行，第三版全过后又改第四版 | 收敛方案：提示词定为第三版不再改；修引用检查（$0 重放全部 606 次读核对）；在 8 月财报上用第三版读一次（约 8 美分，只有\"收错数\"能叫停）；然后做进生产 |\n| 38 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好，按这三步做，你不要再搬石头砸自己脚了，你要收敛上生产\"。第 1 步：引用检查搬进正式代码 `src/hivemind/baseline.py`，加\"行名是报告的、数在同一行就存原文那一整行\"的修复（漏脚注记号、跳格）；对照 36 例全对，三个弄坏的版本各抓出 1 个错；重放全部存下的读法，改变的正好是那 6 个被拒的对数。第 2 步：第三版加修好的检查读 8 月留出财报 132 次（0.096 美元），两人分类 | 第 1 步：只改了那 6 条。第 2 步过：44 个数 0 个错（3 条\"剩余资本开支承诺 70 亿\"两人不一致，对着原文定为\"自己的量，别的期间\"）；v10 3/3、v12 2/3 | 读法定为第三版加修好的检查，不再改；第 3 步写正式代码 |\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib\np=pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json'\\\\'')\nplans=json.loads(p.read_text())\nfor i,q in enumerate(plans):\n print(i,list(q),q.get('\\\\''unit'\\\\''),q.get('\\\\''arm'\\\\''),len(q['\\\\''user'\\\\'']), len(q['\\\\''passages'\\\\'']), list(q['\\\\''family'\\\\'']['\\\\''members'\\\\'']))\nprint(plans[0]['\\\\''system'\\\\''])\nprint(plans[0]['\\\\''user'\\\\''][:2400])\nprint(json.dumps(plans[0]['\\\\''schema'\\\\''],indent=2))\nfor name in ['\\\\''answers'\\\\'','\\\\''retries'\\\\'']:\n p=pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22'\\\\'')/(name+'\\\\''.jsonl'\\\\'')\n for n,l in enumerate(p.open(),1):\n  a=json.loads(l)\n  if a['\\\\''status'\\\\'']=='\\\\''started'\\\\'': continue\n  print(name,n,list(a),{k:v for k,v in a.items() if k not in ('\\\\''data'\\\\'','\\\\''errors'\\\\'') and len(str(v))<1000})\n  print(a['\\\\''errors'\\\\''][:5]);print(a['\\\\''data'\\\\'']['\\\\''figures'\\\\''][:2]);print(a['\\\\''data'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][:1]);break'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib\np=pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan.json'\\\\'')\nplans=json.loads(p.read_text())\nfor i,q in enumerate(plans):\n print(i,list(q),q.get('\\\\''unit'\\\\''),q.get('\\\\''arm'\\\\''),len(q['\\\\''user'\\\\'']), len(q['\\\\''passages'\\\\'']), list(q['\\\\''family'\\\\'']['\\\\''members'\\\\'']))\nprint(plans[0]['\\\\''system'\\\\''])\nprint(plans[0]['\\\\''user'\\\\''][:2400])\nprint(json.dumps(plans[0]['\\\\''schema'\\\\''],indent=2))\nfor name in ['\\\\''answers'\\\\'','\\\\''retries'\\\\'']:\n p=pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22'\\\\'')/(name+'\\\\''.jsonl'\\\\'')\n for n,l in enumerate(p.open(),1):\n  a=json.loads(l)\n  if a['\\\\''status'\\\\'']=='\\\\''started'\\\\'': continue\n  print(name,n,list(a),{k:v for k,v in a.items() if k not in ('\\\\''data'\\\\'','\\\\''errors'\\\\'') and len(str(v))<1000})\n  print(a['\\\\''errors'\\\\''][:5]);print(a['\\\\''data'\\\\'']['\\\\''figures'\\\\''][:2]);print(a['\\\\''data'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][:1]);break'\"","aggregated_output":"0 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] pair-old-1 joint 120154 237 ['v4', 'v8']\n1 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] pair-old-1 separate-v4 119463 237 ['v4']\n2 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] pair-old-1 separate-v8 119571 237 ['v8']\n3 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] pair-old-2 joint 121409 237 ['v14', 'v9']\n4 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] pair-old-2 separate-v14 120696 237 ['v14']\n5 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] pair-old-2 separate-v9 120815 237 ['v9']\n6 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] pair-old-3 joint 134199 260 ['v3', 'v35']\n7 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] pair-old-3 separate-v3 133522 260 ['v3']\n8 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] pair-old-3 separate-v35 133492 260 ['v35']\n9 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] single-O single 23666 35 ['v29']\n10 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] single-S single 17026 31 ['v6']\n11 ['unit', 'arm', 'draw_store', 'members', 'system', 'user', 'schema', 'family', 'passages', 'evidence_ids', 'questions', 'shown'] single-E single 13202 15 ['v41']\nYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n\n- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast per scenario. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n\n[Family] members:\nThesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion.\n\nThesis v8 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Data Center segment revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $400 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $300 billion and less than $350 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $250 billion and less than $300 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is less than $250 billion.\n\nRelations: v8 is part of v4.\nShared quantities, one forecast each per scenario: NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q3; NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q4.\nOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), to be used exactly: v4 = NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027; v8 = NVIDIA | revenue | Data Center | FY2027.\n\n[Evidence in view for the family, 237 items]\n- e111 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85% year over year.\n    Quote: First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion, while non-GAAP earnings per share jumped 140% to $1.87.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e273, e274, e326, e478, e1401, e1425; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1040 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source OpenAI ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 GW of GPUs, a planned deployment that could affect NVIDIA's competitive position in AI accelerators.\n    Quote: OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 gigawatts of GPUs\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the sa\n{\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"figures\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"id\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"what\": {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"population\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              },\n              \"measure\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              },\n              \"scope\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              },\n              \"period\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              }\n            },\n            \"required\": [\n              \"population\",\n              \"measure\",\n              \"scope\",\n              \"period\"\n            ],\n            \"additionalProperties\": false,\n            \"description\": \"What the number measures: population, measure (the quantity, such as revenue or units; never its basis), scope, period.\"\n          },\n          \"basis\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"enum\": [\n              \"consensus\",\n              \"forecast\",\n              \"guidance\",\n              \"reported\",\n              \"third_party\"\n            ],\n            \"description\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's forecast.\"\n          },\n          \"scenario\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"kind\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"enum\": [\n              \"computed\",\n              \"estimate\",\n              \"source\"\n            ],\n            \"description\": \"source: stated in a shown passage; computed: one operation on named figures; estimate: your judgement, stated as yours.