consumer internet companies) should reaccelerate in the fiscal fourth quarter as Vera Rubin supply builds.\\n- e4001 [third_party_view; theguardian.com; original source theguardian.com 2026-09-03; from gnews:774c4b3a90a519fc0d3c9024 2026-09-03] Meta, OpenAI and Microsoft are developing their own AI chips to reduce reliance on NVIDIA processors.\\n    Quote: For Nvidia, building up its open source business may help it cushion a demand slowdown from customers such as \\u2060Meta, OpenAI and Microsoft, which are developing their own AI chips to cut reliance \\u200bon its costly and \\u200bsupply-constrained processors.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4038; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e4202 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:bbd72d5fbff98d39746e6cb9 2026-09-04] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA could reach $40 billion in revenue per gigawatt of computing capacity installed with Vera Rubin, compared with about $25 billion with Blackwell and $18 billion with Hopper.\\n    Quote: Nvidia used to collect about $18 billion in revenue for every gigawatt of computing capacity it helped install with Hopper. With Blackwell, that figure rose to $25 billion. Kress says that with Vera Rubin, the company can reach $40 billion per gigawatt.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2887; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e4248 [party_statement; fool.com (Manali Pradhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:49351bd6de4706de524baf5f 2026-09-05] NVIDIA said it has improved visibility into memory and manufacturing capacity and the land, power, and data-center infrastructure needed for product deployment.\\n    Quote: However, this time, the company has much better visibility into memory and manufacturing capacity, as well as the land, power, and data-center infrastructure needed to deploy its products.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e4220 (); both are in force: NVIDIA warned that shortages of land, power, and other data-center infrastructure could delay customer hardware deployments.\\n- e4274 [third_party_view; calcalistech.com; original source calcalistech.com 2026-09-06; from gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed 2026-09-06] The article estimates NVIDIA FY2027 revenue at about $400 billion, up about 85% year over year.\\n    Quote: This year, however, revenue is expected to jump to about $400 billion, an increase of about 85% from the previous year.\\n- e4278 [third_party_view; calcalistech.com; original source calcalistech.com 2026-09-06; from gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed 2026-09-06] About 92% of NVIDIA revenue comes from data-center infrastructure, including chips and connectivity.\\n    Quote: Approximately 92% of Nvidia\\u2019s revenue comes from data center infrastructure, including chips and connectivity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3797; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e4519 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:fd039f68d65be67fc1005bcc 2026-09-09] NVIDIA's ACIE business\\u2014sovereign, NeoCloud and enterprise Data Center revenue\\u2014grew 138% year over year to $40 billion in fiscal Q2 2027.\\n    Quote: ACIE, the sovereign, NeoCloud and enterprise side of the data center business, grew 138% year over year to $40 billion in fiscal Q2 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2875; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e4803 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ff9cd0726de49630ffec230 2026-09-12] In the second quarter, NVIDIA's ACIE customers grew 25% sequentially versus 13% growth from hyperscale cloud providers, and ACIE represented roughly half of Data Center revenue.\\n    Quote: During the second quarter, the ACIE segment delivered 25% sequential growth (versus 13% from the hyperscale cloud providers) and now represents roughly half of data center revenue.\\n- e4905 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7708a5ba7485f4ee381ccc41 2026-09-14] NVIDIA reported an order backlog of more than $2 trillion in its Q2 release.\\n    Quote: as part of its Q2 release, NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) outlined that its order backlog was more than $2 trillion.\\n- e5064 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan estimated Vera Rubin systems would contribute about 20% of NVIDIA Data Center revenue in Q3 FY2027.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin systems are expected to contribute about one-fifth of Nvidia's Data Center revenue in the third quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3820; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e5068 [third_party_view; Manali Pradhan, CFA; original source Manali Pradhan, CFA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] Manali Pradhan said anticipated Q4 hyperscaler revenue reacceleration could mean less Q3 revenue upside from hyperscalers than from ACIE or other drivers.\\n    Quote: Additionally, since hyperscaler revenue is expected to reaccelerate in the fourth quarter, it may provide less upside to third-quarter revenue results than ACIE or other growth drivers.\\n- e5073 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source University of Manchester team ; from nvblog:uk-air-pollution-research-earth-2 2026-09-16] The University of Manchester team trained NVIDIA Earth-2 CorrDiff on Isambard-AI for UK air-quality forecasting.\\n    Quote: Working with the NVIDIA Earth-2 team, Topping and colleagues generated training data from existing chemistry-climate simulations, then trained Earth-2 CorrDiff \\u2014 a generative downscaling model \\u2014 on Isambard-AI, the U.K.\\u2019s national AI 